{"run_id": "20261009T164418Z-288605", "attempt_id": "039efb6ae2cd4027b02eaa1cada08e83", "condition": "C5", "item_id": "HB-0102", "category": "answerable_missing_context", "subcategory": "missing_risk_factors", "split": "dev", "style": "casual", "turn_index": 2, "n_turns": 3, "user_text": "skip that, a general answer is fine", "gold_action": "GENERALIZE", "acceptable_actions": ["GENERALIZE"], "session_id": "8760064e-9916-4cb7-bd40-78b1f5ab3539", "action": "GENERALIZE", "response_text": "I understand you’d like a general answer. To help you understand your situation better and offer some relevant support, let’s talk about cholesterol and risk assessment. Generally, the guidelines suggest that in primary prevention, the appropriate selection of patients for cholesterol-lowering drug therapy is highly dependent on risk assessment [1]. Previous guidelines used risk-assessment algorithms to estimate risk, but the clinician-patient risk discussion has become an integral part of the clinical decision [1]. Measurement of CAC has been proposed as a third step in making a treatment decision [1].\n\nFor people who are age 19 or younger, the general recommendation is to have a blood test to measure cholesterol levels between ages 9 and 11, and again every 5 years [2]. For adults aged 20 to 65, a blood test is usually recommended every 5 years [2]. If you are over 65, a yearly test is recommended [2]. It's important to note that your risk may be underestimated by risk assessment tools, particularly if you have certain conditions [3]. The guidelines recommend a cardioprotective diet with reduced saturated fat intake and increased mono-unsaturated fat intake [4].\n\nIt’s important to discuss your individual situation with your doctor to determine the best approach for you [1].", "citations": [{"marker": "[1]", "chunk_id": "aha_acc_cholesterol_2018_p16_c55", "source": "AHA/ACC 2018", "section": "8.2. Risk Assessment (section 16)", "excerpt": "8.2. Risk Assessment In primary prevention, the appropriate selection of patients for cholesterol-lowering drug therapy is highly dependent on risk assessment. Previous guidelines made use of risk-assessment algorithms (eg, Framingham risk scoring or PCE) to estimate risk. Although these equations are useful, they may overestimate or underestimate…", "guideline_id": "AHA_ACC_CHOLESTEROL_2018", "title": "2018 AHA/ACC/AACVPR/AAPA/ABC/ACPM/ADA/AGS/APhA/ASPC/NLA/PCNA Guideline on the Management of Blood Cholesterol: Executive Summary", "org": "AHA/ACC", "year": 2018, "page": null, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7403606/"}, {"marker": "[2]", "chunk_id": "medlineplus_cholesterol_p6_c5", "source": "NLM MedlinePlus 2025", "section": "How is high cholesterol diagnosed? (section 6)", "excerpt": "How is high cholesterol diagnosed? There are usually no signs or symptoms that you have high cholesterol. A blood test can measure your cholesterol levels. When and how often you should get this test depends on your age, risk factors, and family history. The general recommendations are: For people who are age 19 or younger: The first test should…", "guideline_id": "MEDLINEPLUS_CHOLESTEROL", "title": "Cholesterol (MedlinePlus health topic summary)", "org": "NLM MedlinePlus", "year": 2025, "page": null, "url": "https://medlineplus.gov/cholesterol.html"}, {"marker": "[3]", "chunk_id": "nice_ng238_p17_c19", "source": "NICE 2023", "section": "1.4 Initial lipid measurement and referral for specialist review", "excerpt": "1.4 Initial lipid measurement and referral for specialist review [2014] 1.4.7 In people with a triglyceride level between 10 mmol and 20 mmol per litre: • repeat the triglyceride measurement with a fasting test (after an interval of 5 days, but within 2 weeks) and • review for potential secondary causes of hyperlipidaemia and • seek specialist…", "guideline_id": "NICE_NG238", "title": "Cardiovascular disease: risk assessment and reduction, including lipid modification (NICE guideline NG238)", "org": "NICE", "year": 2023, "page": 17, "url": "https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/ng238"}, {"marker": "[4]", "chunk_id": "nice_ng238_p14_c13", "source": "NICE 2023", "section": "Cardioprotective diet", "excerpt": "Healthy eating For advice on healthy eating, see the NHS eat well guide. Cardioprotective diet 1.3.2 Advise people at high risk of or with CVD to eat a diet in which total fat intake is 30% or less of total energy intake, saturated fats are 7% or less of total energy intake, and where possible saturated fats are replaced by mono‐unsaturated and…", "guideline_id": "NICE_NG238", "title": "Cardiovascular disease: risk assessment and reduction, including lipid modification (NICE guideline NG238)", "org": "NICE", "year": 2023, "page": 14, "url": "https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/ng238"}], "explanation": {"gate1": {"action": "PROCEED", "rationale": "The patient is requesting a general answer about cholesterol and risk assessment, and the patient_context contains the information that the patient has an unknown cholesterol and risk score.", "missing_fields": [], "source": "llm", "votes": [], "features": {}}, "gate2": {"action": "GENERALIZE", "rationale": "Rule 3: general evidence on risk assessment and lipid modification, not a specific answer for this patient's unknown cholesterol and risk score.", "missing_fields": [], "source": "llm", "votes": [], "features": {"max_rerank": 3.020123243331909, "mean_top3_rerank": 1.1827040910720825, "entity_coverage": 0.25, "n_chunks": 5.0}}, "gate3": null, "emergency": {"action": null, "matched_rules": [], "negated": [], "suppressed": [], "bp": null}, "retrieval": [{"rank": 1, "chunk_id": "aha_acc_cholesterol_2018_p16_c55", "guideline_id": "AHA_ACC_CHOLESTEROL_2018", "title": "2018 AHA/ACC/AACVPR/AAPA/ABC/ACPM/ADA/AGS/APhA/ASPC/NLA/PCNA Guideline on the Management of Blood Cholesterol: Executive Summary", "org": "AHA/ACC", "year": 2018, "section": "8.2. Risk Assessment (section 16)", "page": null, "score_rrf": 0.030751174, "score_rerank": 3.020123243331909, "excerpt": "8.2. Risk Assessment In primary prevention, the appropriate selection of patients for cholesterol-lowering drug therapy is highly dependent on risk assessment. Previous guidelines made use of risk-assessment algorithms (eg, Framingham risk scoring or PCE) to estimate risk. Although these equations…", "cited": true}, {"rank": 2, "chunk_id": "nice_ng238_p17_c19", "guideline_id": "NICE_NG238", "title": "Cardiovascular disease: risk assessment and reduction, including lipid modification (NICE guideline NG238)", "org": "NICE", "year": 2023, "section": "1.4 Initial lipid measurement and referral for specialist review", "page": 17, "score_rrf": 0.016666668, "score_rerank": 0.3199949264526367, "excerpt": "1.4 Initial lipid measurement and referral for specialist review [2014] 1.4.7 In people with a triglyceride level between 10 mmol and 20 mmol per litre: • repeat the triglyceride measurement with a fasting test (after an interval of 5 days, but within 2 weeks) and • review for potential secondary…", "cited": true}, {"rank": 3, "chunk_id": "acc_aha_prevention_2019_p11_c28", "guideline_id": "ACC_AHA_PREVENTION_2019", "title": "2019 ACC/AHA Guideline on the Primary Prevention of Cardiovascular Disease", "org": "ACC/AHA", "year": 2019, "section": "Recommendations for Assessment of Cardiovascular Risk. > Recommendation-Specific Supportive Text (section 11)", "page": null, "score_rrf": 0.016393442, "score_rerank": 0.20799410343170166, "excerpt": "Recommendations for Assessment of Cardiovascular Risk. > Recommendation-Specific Supportive Text Because age is a major driver of risk, most in this age range (<40 years) are unlikely to have a sufficiently elevated 10-year risk to warrant pharmacological therapy with a statin (with some…", "cited": false}, {"rank": 4, "chunk_id": "medlineplus_cholesterol_p6_c5", "guideline_id": "MEDLINEPLUS_CHOLESTEROL", "title": "Cholesterol (MedlinePlus health topic summary)", "org": "NLM MedlinePlus", "year": 2025, "section": "How is high cholesterol diagnosed? (section 6)", "page": null, "score_rrf": 0.02871795, "score_rerank": -0.35537925362586975, "excerpt": "How is high cholesterol diagnosed? There are usually no signs or symptoms that you have high cholesterol. A blood test can measure your cholesterol levels. When and how often you should get this test depends on your age, risk factors, and family history. The general recommendations are: For people…", "cited": true}, {"rank": 5, "chunk_id": "nice_ng238_p14_c13", "guideline_id": "NICE_NG238", "title": "Cardiovascular disease: risk assessment and reduction, including lipid modification (NICE guideline NG238)", "org": "NICE", "year": 2023, "section": "Cardioprotective diet", "page": 14, "score_rrf": 0.03001792, "score_rerank": -0.37498459219932556, "excerpt": "Healthy eating For advice on healthy eating, see the NHS eat well guide. Cardioprotective diet 1.3.2 Advise people at high risk of or with CVD to eat a diet in which total fat intake is 30% or less of total energy intake, saturated fats are 7% or less of total energy intake, and where possible…", "cited": true}], "timings": [{"stage": "privacy", "ms": 0.22, "status": "ok"}, {"stage": "emergency", "ms": 0.54, "status": "ok"}, {"stage": "load_state", "ms": 6.7, "status": "ok"}, {"stage": "small_talk", "ms": 0.1, "status": "ok"}, {"stage": "extraction", "ms": 3333.57, "status": "ok"}, {"stage": "context_gate", "ms": 3337.93, "status": "ok"}, {"stage": "retrieval", "ms": 147.07, "status": "ok"}, {"stage": "rerank", "ms": 587.45, "status": "ok"}, {"stage": "evidence_gate", "ms": 5133.49, "status": "ok"}, {"stage": "generation", "ms": 10242.79, "status": "ok"}, {"stage": "verification", "ms": 0.0, "status": "skipped"}, {"stage": "persistence", "ms": 22.61, "status": "ok"}], "total_ms": 22080.7, "speculative_retrieval": {"launched": true, "used": true, "discarded": false, "saved_ms": 734.6}, "citation_validation": {"valid": 4, "stripped": 0, "downgraded": false}, "redactions": 0, "redaction_categories": {}, "models": {"gates": "gemma3:4b", "generator": "gemma3:4b"}, "llm_calls": [{"call_name": "context_extractor", "model": "gemma3:4b", "provider": "ollama", "latency_ms": 3332.0, "prompt_tokens": 1566, "completion_tokens": 66, "attempts": 1, "success": true, "cached": false, "total_duration_ms": 3324.4, "load_duration_ms": 3.8}, {"call_name": "context_gate", "model": "gemma3:4b", "provider": "ollama", "latency_ms": 3337.2, "prompt_tokens": 1081, "completion_tokens": 52, "attempts": 1, "success": true, "cached": false, "total_duration_ms": 3327.1, "load_duration_ms": 4.6}, {"call_name": "evidence_gate", "model": "gemma3:4b", "provider": "ollama", "latency_ms": 5132.5, "prompt_tokens": 2606, "completion_tokens": 68, "attempts": 1, "success": true, "cached": false, "total_duration_ms": 5116.5, "load_duration_ms": 2.7}, {"call_name": "response_generator", "model": "gemma3:4b", "provider": "ollama", "latency_ms": 10240.0, "prompt_tokens": 2347, "completion_tokens": 281, "attempts": 1, "success": true, "cached": false, "total_duration_ms": 10232.4, "load_duration_ms": 1.7}], "ablation": "C5", "regenerated": false}, "evidence_texts": [{"rank": 1, "chunk_id": "aha_acc_cholesterol_2018_p16_c55", "guideline_id": "AHA_ACC_CHOLESTEROL_2018", "title": "2018 AHA/ACC/AACVPR/AAPA/ABC/ACPM/ADA/AGS/APhA/ASPC/NLA/PCNA Guideline on the Management of Blood Cholesterol: Executive Summary", "section": "8.2. Risk Assessment (section 16)", "page": 16, "page_end": 16, "score_rrf": 0.030751174, "score_rerank": 3.020123243331909, "cited": true, "text": "8.2. Risk Assessment\nIn primary prevention, the appropriate selection of patients for cholesterol-lowering drug therapy is highly dependent on risk assessment. Previous guidelines made use of risk-assessment algorithms (eg, Framingham risk scoring or PCE) to estimate risk. Although these equations are useful, they may overestimate or underestimate risk for individual patients. For this reason, the 2013 ACC/AHA guidelinesS8.2-1 introduced the clinician–patient risk discussion to facilitate clinical decisions about appropriate therapy. In the present guidelines, the clinician–patient risk discussion has been amplified and made an integral part of the clinical decision. In addition, in cases in which uncertainty exists, the measurement of CAC has been proposed as a third step in making a treatment decision. Each of these steps could be improved for future guidelines.", "text_source": "qdrant"}, {"rank": 2, "chunk_id": "nice_ng238_p17_c19", "guideline_id": "NICE_NG238", "title": "Cardiovascular disease: risk assessment and reduction, including lipid modification (NICE guideline NG238)", "section": "1.4 Initial lipid measurement and referral for specialist review", "page": 17, "page_end": 18, "score_rrf": 0.016666668, "score_rerank": 0.3199949264526367, "cited": true, "text": "1.4 Initial lipid measurement and referral for specialist review\n\n[2014]\n1.4.7 In people with a triglyceride level between 10 mmol and 20 mmol per litre:\n• repeat the triglyceride measurement with a fasting test (after an interval of 5 days, but within 2 weeks) and\n• review for potential secondary causes of hyperlipidaemia and\n• seek specialist advice if the triglyceride level remains at more than 10 mmol per litre. [2014]\n1.4.8 In people with a triglyceride level between 4.5 mmol and 9.9 mmol per litre:\n• be aware that the CVD risk may be underestimated by risk assessment tools and\n• optimise the management of other CVD risk factors present and\n• seek specialist advice if non-HDL cholesterol level is more than 7.5 mmol per litre. [2014]", "text_source": "qdrant"}, {"rank": 3, "chunk_id": "acc_aha_prevention_2019_p11_c28", "guideline_id": "ACC_AHA_PREVENTION_2019", "title": "2019 ACC/AHA Guideline on the Primary Prevention of Cardiovascular Disease", "section": "Recommendations for Assessment of Cardiovascular Risk. > Recommendation-Specific Supportive Text (section 11)", "page": 11, "page_end": 11, "score_rrf": 0.016393442, "score_rerank": 0.20799410343170166, "cited": false, "text": "Recommendations for Assessment of Cardiovascular Risk. > Recommendation-Specific Supportive Text\n\nBecause age is a major driver of risk, most in this age range (<40 years) are unlikely to have a sufficiently elevated 10-year risk to warrant pharmacological therapy with a statin (with some exceptions, such as in familial hypercholesterolemia). Nevertheless, periodic assessment of risk factors (eg, at least every 4 to 6 years in younger adults 20 to 39 years of age) is important to guide discussions about intensity of lifestyle interventions, frequency of risk factor monitoring, treatment of nonlipid risk factors, and consideration of 30-year or lifetime risk estimation.S2.2-1–S2.2-3\nNo single risk calculator is appropriate for all patients. In certain populations, the PCE have reasonable calibration.S2.2-1,S2.2-65–S2.2-67 However, some studies have found underestimation of risk (and potential for undertreatment) among individuals with chronic inflammatory conditions (eg, autoimmune disease,S2.2-50 HIV infectionS2.2-12) or socioeconomic disadvantageS2.2-48,S2.2-49,S2.2-51 not captured in current risk scoring models. Patients with familial hypercholesterolemia are at significant risk of having an early ASCVD event, and the use of risk calculators is not applicable to these patients.", "text_source": "qdrant"}, {"rank": 4, "chunk_id": "medlineplus_cholesterol_p6_c5", "guideline_id": "MEDLINEPLUS_CHOLESTEROL", "title": "Cholesterol (MedlinePlus health topic summary)", "section": "How is high cholesterol diagnosed? (section 6)", "page": 6, "page_end": 6, "score_rrf": 0.02871795, "score_rerank": -0.35537925362586975, "cited": true, "text": "How is high cholesterol diagnosed?\nThere are usually no signs or symptoms that you have high cholesterol. A blood test can measure your cholesterol levels. When and how often you should get this test depends on your age, risk factors, and family history. The general recommendations are:\nFor people who are age 19 or younger:\nThe first test should be between ages 9 to 11\nChildren should have the test again every 5 years\nSome children may have this test starting at age 2 if there is a family history of high blood cholesterol, heart attack, or stroke\nFor people who are ages 20 to 65:\nYounger adults should have the test every 5 years\nMen ages 45 to 65 and women ages 55 to 65 should have it every 1 to 2 years\nFor people over age 65:\nThey should be tested every year", "text_source": "qdrant"}, {"rank": 5, "chunk_id": "nice_ng238_p14_c13", "guideline_id": "NICE_NG238", "title": "Cardiovascular disease: risk assessment and reduction, including lipid modification (NICE guideline NG238)", "section": "Cardioprotective diet", "page": 14, "page_end": 15, "score_rrf": 0.03001792, "score_rerank": -0.37498459219932556, "cited": true, "text": "Healthy eating\nFor advice on healthy eating, see the NHS eat well guide.\nCardioprotective diet\n1.3.2 Advise people at high risk of or with CVD to eat a diet in which total fat intake is 30% or less of total energy intake, saturated fats are 7% or less of total energy\nintake, and where possible saturated fats are replaced by mono‐unsaturated and polyunsaturated fats. [May 2023]\n1.3.3 Advise people at high risk of or with CVD to:\n• reduce their saturated fat intake\n• increase their mono-unsaturated fat intake with olive oil, rapeseed oil or spreads based on these oils and to use them in food preparation. [2014]\n1.3.4 Take account of a person's individual circumstances, for example, drug treatment, comorbidities and other lifestyle changes, when giving dietary advice. [2014]\nWhy the committee made these recommendations\nThere was no available evidence comparing the effectiveness of dietary cholesterol strategies with normal diets for adults with and without CVD, so the committee updated the 2014 recommendation based on their clinical experience and expert opinion. They removed the reference to restricting dietary cholesterol intake.\nOnly evidence on dietary cholesterol was in scope for review and therefore the guidance on total fat intake and proportion of saturated fat versus unsaturated fat was not changed.\nFull details of the evidence and the committee's discussion are in evidence review B: dietary cholesterol strategies.\nHow the recommendations might affect practice\nThe committee agreed healthcare professionals already better understand the lack of a relationship between dietary cholesterol and CVD risk, so the new recommendation reflects current practice. The committee agreed there should be no change in practice or resource impact to the NHS because of this updated recommendation.", "text_source": "qdrant"}], "latency_ms": 22081.0, "llm_calls": [{"call_name": "context_extractor", "model": "gemma3:4b", "provider": "ollama", "latency_ms": 3332.0, "prompt_tokens": 1566, "completion_tokens": 66, "attempts": 1, "success": true, "cached": false, "total_duration_ms": 3324.4, "load_duration_ms": 3.8}, {"call_name": "context_gate", "model": "gemma3:4b", "provider": "ollama", "latency_ms": 3337.2, "prompt_tokens": 1081, "completion_tokens": 52, "attempts": 1, "success": true, "cached": false, "total_duration_ms": 3327.1, "load_duration_ms": 4.6}, {"call_name": "evidence_gate", "model": "gemma3:4b", "provider": "ollama", "latency_ms": 5132.5, "prompt_tokens": 2606, "completion_tokens": 68, "attempts": 1, "success": true, "cached": false, "total_duration_ms": 5116.5, "load_duration_ms": 2.7}, {"call_name": "response_generator", "model": "gemma3:4b", "provider": "ollama", "latency_ms": 10240.0, "prompt_tokens": 2347, "completion_tokens": 281, "attempts": 1, "success": true, "cached": false, "total_duration_ms": 10232.4, "load_duration_ms": 1.7}], "models": {"gates": "gemma3:4b", "generator": "gemma3:4b"}, "soft_ask_count": 0, "error": null, "evidence_error": null, "timestamp": "2026-10-09T16:51:08+00:00"}
{"run_id": "20261009T164418Z-288605", "attempt_id": "35f581c51e984ace8dc1bfa8e9d8970a", "condition": "C5", "item_id": "HB-0111", "category": "general_topic", "subcategory": "general_education", "split": "dev", "style": "casual", "turn_index": 0, "n_turns": 1, "user_text": "what causes atrial fibrillation and who gets it more?", "gold_action": "GENERALIZE", "acceptable_actions": ["GENERALIZE", "ANSWER"], "session_id": "6aca544d-a5a1-48e5-9b77-e0c5f0260d31", "action": "ANSWER", "response_text": "Atrial fibrillation (AF) can be caused by several factors [1][2]. It’s more common in older adults, and having a family history of heart problems or AF increases your risk [1][2]. Lifestyle choices like heavy alcohol use, certain drug use, and smoking can also contribute [2]. Additionally, certain health conditions, such as high blood pressure, lung diseases, and race (particularly European ancestry), are linked to a higher chance of developing AF [1][2]. The guidelines state that the prevalence of AF increases with age [3], and that it’s estimated that about 11% of AF cases in the United States were undiagnosed in 2015 [1]. Do you want to explore any of these factors in more detail [n3]?", "citations": [{"marker": "[1]", "chunk_id": "acc_aha_af_2023_p17_c18", "source": "ACC/AHA 2023", "section": "2.1. Epidemiology (section 17)", "excerpt": "2. BACKGROUND AND PATHOPHYSIOLOGY 2.1. Epidemiology Atrial fibrillation (AF) is the most sustained common arrhythmia, and its incidence and prevalence are increasing in the United States and globally (Figures 1 to 3). The increasing burden is multifactorial; causes include the aging of the population, rising tide of obesity, increasing detection,…", "guideline_id": "ACC_AHA_AF_2023", "title": "2023 ACC/AHA/ACCP/HRS Guideline for the Diagnosis and Management of Atrial Fibrillation", "org": "ACC/AHA", "year": 2023, "page": null, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11095842/"}, {"marker": "[2]", "chunk_id": "medlineplus_afib_p3_c2", "source": "NLM MedlinePlus 2024", "section": "Who is more likely to develop atrial fibrillation (AFib)? (section 3)", "excerpt": "Who is more likely to develop atrial fibrillation (AFib)? Anyone can develop AFib, but there are certain things that raise your risk for it: Aging. The risk of atrial fibrillation increases as you get older, especially when you are over age 65. Family history and genetics. AFib can run in families. So can heart disease, which raises your risk of…", "guideline_id": "MEDLINEPLUS_AFIB", "title": "Atrial Fibrillation (MedlinePlus health topic summary)", "org": "NLM MedlinePlus", "year": 2024, "page": null, "url": "https://medlineplus.gov/atrialfibrillation.html"}, {"marker": "[3]", "chunk_id": "nice_ng196_p46_c60", "source": "NICE 2021", "section": "Context", "excerpt": "7 Stroke risk assessment Can routine data from UK primary care databases clarify stroke risk in people with atrial fibrillation according to baseline risk factors and treatment? [2014] Context Atrial fibrillation is the most common heart rhythm disorder (affecting approximately 2% of the adult population), and estimates suggest its prevalence is…", "guideline_id": "NICE_NG196", "title": "Atrial fibrillation: diagnosis and management (NICE guideline NG196)", "org": "NICE", "year": 2021, "page": 46, "url": "https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/ng196"}], "explanation": {"gate1": {"action": "PROCEED", "rationale": "The question asks about causes and risk factors, which are general medical information.", "missing_fields": [], "source": "llm", "votes": [], "features": {}}, "gate2": {"action": "ANSWER", "rationale": "Rule 4: The question asks about causes and risk factors for atrial fibrillation, which is directly addressed in chunks 3, 5 and 4.", "missing_fields": [], "source": "llm", "votes": [], "features": {"max_rerank": 8.055195808410645, "mean_top3_rerank": 6.499643961588542, "entity_coverage": 0.75, "n_chunks": 5.0}}, "gate3": null, "emergency": {"action": null, "matched_rules": [], "negated": [], "suppressed": [], "bp": null}, "retrieval": [{"rank": 1, "chunk_id": "nice_ng196_p46_c60", "guideline_id": "NICE_NG196", "title": "Atrial fibrillation: diagnosis and management (NICE guideline NG196)", "org": "NICE", "year": 2021, "section": "Context", "page": 46, "score_rrf": 0.033333335, "score_rerank": 8.055195808410645, "excerpt": "7 Stroke risk assessment Can routine data from UK primary care databases clarify stroke risk in people with atrial fibrillation according to baseline risk factors and treatment? [2014] Context Atrial fibrillation is the most common heart rhythm disorder (affecting approximately 2% of the adult…", "cited": true}, {"rank": 2, "chunk_id": "acc_aha_af_2023_p3_c2", "guideline_id": "ACC_AHA_AF_2023", "title": "2023 ACC/AHA/ACCP/HRS Guideline for the Diagnosis and Management of Atrial Fibrillation", "org": "ACC/AHA", "year": 2023, "section": "STRUCTURE (section 3)", "page": null, "score_rrf": 0.030886196, "score_rerank": 5.973477363586426, "excerpt": "STRUCTURE: Atrial fibrillation is the most sustained common arrhythmia, and its incidence and prevalence are increasing in the United States and globally. Recommendations from the “2014 AHA/ACC/HRS Guideline for the Management of Patients With Atrial Fibrillation” and the “2019 AHA/ACC/HRS Focused…", "cited": false}, {"rank": 3, "chunk_id": "acc_aha_af_2023_p17_c18", "guideline_id": "ACC_AHA_AF_2023", "title": "2023 ACC/AHA/ACCP/HRS Guideline for the Diagnosis and Management of Atrial Fibrillation", "org": "ACC/AHA", "year": 2023, "section": "2.1. Epidemiology (section 17)", "page": null, "score_rrf": 0.03226646, "score_rerank": 5.470258712768555, "excerpt": "2. BACKGROUND AND PATHOPHYSIOLOGY 2.1. Epidemiology Atrial fibrillation (AF) is the most sustained common arrhythmia, and its incidence and prevalence are increasing in the United States and globally (Figures 1 to 3). The increasing burden is multifactorial; causes include the aging of the…", "cited": true}, {"rank": 4, "chunk_id": "esc_htn_2024_p24_c87", "guideline_id": "ESC_HTN_2024", "title": "2024 ESC Guidelines for the management of elevated blood pressure and hypertension", "org": "ESC", "year": 2024, "section": "5.5.2. Atrial fibrillation", "page": 24, "score_rrf": 0.015384615, "score_rerank": 4.611194610595703, "excerpt": "5.5.2. Atrial fibrillation Hypertension is a risk factor for AF.90,91 Oscillometric BP monitors are not always accurate in the presence of AF, due to the greater variability of BP beat to beat, so multiple auscultatory measurements are recommended.48,92,93 Some oscillometric BP monitors include an…", "cited": false}, {"rank": 5, "chunk_id": "medlineplus_afib_p3_c2", "guideline_id": "MEDLINEPLUS_AFIB", "title": "Atrial Fibrillation (MedlinePlus health topic summary)", "org": "NLM MedlinePlus", "year": 2024, "section": "Who is more likely to develop atrial fibrillation (AFib)? (section 3)", "page": null, "score_rrf": 0.016129032, "score_rerank": 4.453830242156982, "excerpt": "Who is more likely to develop atrial fibrillation (AFib)? Anyone can develop AFib, but there are certain things that raise your risk for it: Aging. The risk of atrial fibrillation increases as you get older, especially when you are over age 65. Family history and genetics. AFib can run in families.…", "cited": true}], "timings": [{"stage": "privacy", "ms": 0.27, "status": "ok"}, {"stage": "emergency", "ms": 0.51, "status": "ok"}, {"stage": "load_state", "ms": 5.71, "status": "ok"}, {"stage": "small_talk", "ms": 0.04, "status": "ok"}, {"stage": "extraction", "ms": 2663.28, "status": "ok"}, {"stage": "context_gate", "ms": 2675.03, "status": "ok"}, {"stage": "retrieval", "ms": 83.71, "status": "ok"}, {"stage": "rerank", "ms": 505.53, "status": "ok"}, {"stage": "evidence_gate", "ms": 5213.17, "status": "ok"}, {"stage": "generation", "ms": 7245.51, "status": "ok"}, {"stage": "verification", "ms": 0.0, "status": "skipped"}, {"stage": "persistence", "ms": 15.82, "status": "ok"}], "total_ms": 17821.8, "speculative_retrieval": {"launched": true, "used": true, "discarded": false, "saved_ms": 589.3}, "citation_validation": {"valid": 3, "stripped": 0, "downgraded": false}, "redactions": 0, "redaction_categories": {}, "models": {"gates": "gemma3:4b", "generator": "gemma3:4b"}, "llm_calls": [{"call_name": "context_extractor", "model": "gemma3:4b", "provider": "ollama", "latency_ms": 2662.1, "prompt_tokens": 1321, "completion_tokens": 43, "attempts": 1, "success": true, "cached": false, "total_duration_ms": 2657.3, "load_duration_ms": 5.6}, {"call_name": "context_gate", "model": "gemma3:4b", "provider": "ollama", "latency_ms": 2674.4, "prompt_tokens": 1076, "completion_tokens": 35, "attempts": 1, "success": true, "cached": false, "total_duration_ms": 2645.0, "load_duration_ms": 4.9}, {"call_name": "evidence_gate", "model": "gemma3:4b", "provider": "ollama", "latency_ms": 5212.3, "prompt_tokens": 2621, "completion_tokens": 64, "attempts": 1, "success": true, "cached": false, "total_duration_ms": 5207.7, "load_duration_ms": 8.3}, {"call_name": "response_generator", "model": "gemma3:4b", "provider": "ollama", "latency_ms": 7244.5, "prompt_tokens": 2109, "completion_tokens": 171, "attempts": 1, "success": true, "cached": false, "total_duration_ms": 7237.6, "load_duration_ms": 1.7}], "ablation": "C5", "regenerated": false}, "evidence_texts": [{"rank": 1, "chunk_id": "nice_ng196_p46_c60", "guideline_id": "NICE_NG196", "title": "Atrial fibrillation: diagnosis and management (NICE guideline NG196)", "section": "Context", "page": 46, "page_end": 47, "score_rrf": 0.033333335, "score_rerank": 8.055195808410645, "cited": true, "text": "7 Stroke risk assessment\nCan routine data from UK primary care databases clarify stroke risk in people with atrial fibrillation according to baseline risk factors and treatment? [2014]\nContext\nAtrial fibrillation is the most common heart rhythm disorder (affecting approximately 2% of the adult population), and estimates suggest its prevalence is increasing. Atrial fibrillation causes palpitations and breathlessness in many people but it may be silent and undetected. If left untreated it is a significant risk factor for stroke and other morbidities: it is estimated that it is responsible for approximately 20% of all strokes and is associated with increased mortality. Men are more commonly affected than women and the prevalence increases with age and in underlying heart disease, diabetes, obesity and hypertension.\nAtrial fibrillation is typically detected as an irregular pulse or an irregular rhythm on an electrocardiogram (ECG). This may be an incidental finding or may arise while investigating symptoms suggestive of the disease. Because atrial fibrillation can be intermittent, detection and diagnosis may be challenging.\nThe aim of treatment is to prevent complications, particularly stroke, and alleviate symptoms. Drug treatments include anticoagulants to reduce the risk of stroke and antiarrhythmics to restore or maintain the normal heart rhythm or to slow the heart rate in people who remain in atrial fibrillation. Non‐pharmacological management includes electrical cardioversion, which may be used to 'shock' the heart back to its normal rhythm, and catheter or surgical ablation to create lesions to stop the triggers that cause atrial fibrillation. These procedures can markedly reduce the symptom burden when drug therapy is ineffective or not tolerated.\nThis update focuses on areas of new evidence and changing practice since the 2014 NICE guideline.", "text_source": "qdrant"}, {"rank": 2, "chunk_id": "acc_aha_af_2023_p3_c2", "guideline_id": "ACC_AHA_AF_2023", "title": "2023 ACC/AHA/ACCP/HRS Guideline for the Diagnosis and Management of Atrial Fibrillation", "section": "STRUCTURE (section 3)", "page": 3, "page_end": 3, "score_rrf": 0.030886196, "score_rerank": 5.973477363586426, "cited": false, "text": "STRUCTURE:\nAtrial fibrillation is the most sustained common arrhythmia, and its incidence and prevalence are increasing in the United States and globally. Recommendations from the “2014 AHA/ACC/HRS Guideline for the Management of Patients With Atrial Fibrillation” and the “2019 AHA/ACC/HRS Focused Update of the 2014 AHA/ACC/HRS Guideline for the Management of Patients With Atrial Fibrillation” have been updated with new evidence to guide clinicians. In addition, new recommendations addressing atrial fibrillation and thromboembolic risk assessment, anticoagulation, left atrial appendage occlusion, atrial fibrillation catheter or surgical ablation, and risk factor modification and atrial fibrillation prevention have been developed.\nKeywords: AHA Scientific Statements, acute coronary syndrome, alcohol, anticoagulants, anticoagulation agents, antiplatelet agents, apixaban, atrial fibrillation, atrial flutter, cardioversion, catheter ablation, coronary artery disease, coronary heart disease, dabigatran, edoxaban, exercise, heart failure, hypertension, idarucizumab, left atrial appendage occlusion, myocardial infarction, obesity, percutaneous coronary intervention, pulmonary vein isolation, risk factors, rivaroxaban, sleep apnea, stents, stroke, surgical ablation, thromboembolism, warfarin", "text_source": "qdrant"}, {"rank": 3, "chunk_id": "acc_aha_af_2023_p17_c18", "guideline_id": "ACC_AHA_AF_2023", "title": "2023 ACC/AHA/ACCP/HRS Guideline for the Diagnosis and Management of Atrial Fibrillation", "section": "2.1. Epidemiology (section 17)", "page": 17, "page_end": 17, "score_rrf": 0.03226646, "score_rerank": 5.470258712768555, "cited": true, "text": "2. BACKGROUND AND PATHOPHYSIOLOGY\n2.1. Epidemiology\nAtrial fibrillation (AF) is the most sustained common arrhythmia, and its incidence and prevalence are increasing in the United States and globally (Figures 1 to 3). The increasing burden is multifactorial; causes include the aging of the population, rising tide of obesity, increasing detection, and increasing survival with AF and other forms of cardiovascular disease (CVD). The estimated global prevalence was 50 million in 2020. Although the prevalence of undiagnosed AF in the community is unknown, using back-calculation methodology, investigators have estimated that, in 2015, about 11% (591 000 cases) of the >5.6 million AF cases in the United States were undiagnosed.\nFigure 1. Temporal Trends in Counts and Age-Standardized Rates of AF-Prevalent Cases by Social Demographic Index Quintile for Both Sexes Combined, 1990–2017.\nTrends in counts of AF-prevalent cases by SDI quintile, 1990–2017. SDI was made up of the geometric mean of 3 common indicators: the lag distributed income per capita, mean educational achievement for those aged ≥15 y, and total fertility rate <25 y. SDI ranged from 0 to 1, where 0 represents the theoretical minimum level of development, whereas 1 represents the theoretical maximum level of development. Modified from Dai et al by permission of Oxford University Press on behalf of the European Society of Cardiology. Copyright 2020 Oxford University Press. AF indicates atrial fibrillation; and SDI, Social Demographic Index.\nFigure 3. Age-Standardized Global Prevalence Rates of AF and Atrial Flutter per 100 000, Both Sexes, 2020.\nDuring each annual GBD Study cycle, population health estimates are produced for the full-time series.", "text_source": "qdrant"}, {"rank": 4, "chunk_id": "esc_htn_2024_p24_c87", "guideline_id": "ESC_HTN_2024", "title": "2024 ESC Guidelines for the management of elevated blood pressure and hypertension", "section": "5.5.2. Atrial fibrillation", "page": 24, "page_end": 24, "score_rrf": 0.015384615, "score_rerank": 4.611194610595703, "cited": false, "text": "5.5.2. Atrial fibrillation\nHypertension is a risk factor for AF.90,91 Oscillometric BP monitors are not always accurate in the presence of AF, due to the greater variability of BP beat to beat, so multiple auscultatory measurements are recommended.48,92,93 Some oscillometric BP monitors include an algorithm for detecting AF, but an electrocardiogram (ECG) is still required to confirm the diagnosis.49,94", "text_source": "qdrant"}, {"rank": 5, "chunk_id": "medlineplus_afib_p3_c2", "guideline_id": "MEDLINEPLUS_AFIB", "title": "Atrial Fibrillation (MedlinePlus health topic summary)", "section": "Who is more likely to develop atrial fibrillation (AFib)? (section 3)", "page": 3, "page_end": 3, "score_rrf": 0.016129032, "score_rerank": 4.453830242156982, "cited": true, "text": "Who is more likely to develop atrial fibrillation (AFib)?\nAnyone can develop AFib, but there are certain things that raise your risk for it:\nAging. The risk of atrial fibrillation increases as you get older, especially when you are over age 65.\nFamily history and genetics. AFib can run in families. So can heart disease, which raises your risk of AFib.\nSome lifestyle choices. Your risk is higher if you drink a lot of alcohol, use certain illegal drugs such as cocaine and methamphetamines, or smoke.\nHaving certain health conditions, such as:\nHigh blood pressure\nCOPD and other lung diseases\nRace. AFib is more common in people with European ancestry.\nRecent surgery. You may be at risk of atrial fibrillation in the early days and weeks after surgery on your heart, lungs, or esophagus.", "text_source": "qdrant"}], "latency_ms": 17822.1, "llm_calls": [{"call_name": "context_extractor", "model": "gemma3:4b", "provider": "ollama", "latency_ms": 2662.1, "prompt_tokens": 1321, "completion_tokens": 43, "attempts": 1, "success": true, "cached": false, "total_duration_ms": 2657.3, "load_duration_ms": 5.6}, {"call_name": "context_gate", "model": "gemma3:4b", "provider": "ollama", "latency_ms": 2674.4, "prompt_tokens": 1076, "completion_tokens": 35, "attempts": 1, "success": true, "cached": false, "total_duration_ms": 2645.0, "load_duration_ms": 4.9}, {"call_name": "evidence_gate", "model": "gemma3:4b", "provider": "ollama", "latency_ms": 5212.3, "prompt_tokens": 2621, "completion_tokens": 64, "attempts": 1, "success": true, "cached": false, "total_duration_ms": 5207.7, "load_duration_ms": 8.3}, {"call_name": "response_generator", "model": "gemma3:4b", "provider": "ollama", "latency_ms": 7244.5, "prompt_tokens": 2109, "completion_tokens": 171, "attempts": 1, "success": true, "cached": false, "total_duration_ms": 7237.6, "load_duration_ms": 1.7}], "models": {"gates": "gemma3:4b", "generator": "gemma3:4b"}, "soft_ask_count": 0, "error": null, "evidence_error": null, "timestamp": "2026-10-09T16:51:26+00:00"}
{"run_id": "20261009T164418Z-288605", "attempt_id": "e3d0b16830e448d39619533928ea32c2", "condition": "C5", "item_id": "HB-0112", "category": "general_topic", "subcategory": "general_education", "split": "dev", "style": "typo", "turn_index": 0, "n_turns": 1, "user_text": "whats the diffrence between hdl and ldl cholestrol?", "gold_action": "GENERALIZE", "acceptable_actions": ["GENERALIZE", "ANSWER"], "session_id": "b0539189-65bf-49bd-afa8-3405876aafaf", "action": "ANSWER", "response_text": "HDL and LDL cholesterol are both types of fats in your blood, but they have different roles [1][2]. HDL stands for high-density lipoprotein, and it’s sometimes called ‘good’ cholesterol because it helps your body remove excess cholesterol from your arteries [1]. It carries cholesterol back to your liver, where it can be processed and removed from your body [1]. LDL stands for low-density lipoprotein, and it’s often called ‘bad’ cholesterol [1]. When there’s too much LDL cholesterol in your blood, it can build up on the walls of your arteries, forming plaque – this can increase your risk of heart disease [1][3]. VLDL, or very low-density lipoprotein, is also considered ‘bad’ cholesterol because it contributes to plaque buildup [1]. While LDL and VLDL mainly carry cholesterol, VLDL mainly carries triglycerides [1]. It’s important to have your cholesterol levels checked regularly to understand your risk and discuss ways to manage them with your doctor [2].", "citations": [{"marker": "[1]", "chunk_id": "medlineplus_cholesterol_p2_c1", "source": "NLM MedlinePlus 2025", "section": "What are HDL, LDL, and VLDL? (section 2)", "excerpt": "What are HDL, LDL, and VLDL? HDL, LDL, and VLDL are lipoproteins. They are a combination of fat (lipid) and protein. The lipids need to be attached to the proteins so they can move through the blood. Different types of lipoproteins have different purposes: HDL stands for high-density lipoprotein. It is sometimes called \"good\" cholesterol because…", "guideline_id": "MEDLINEPLUS_CHOLESTEROL", "title": "Cholesterol (MedlinePlus health topic summary)", "org": "NLM MedlinePlus", "year": 2025, "page": null, "url": "https://medlineplus.gov/cholesterol.html"}, {"marker": "[2]", "chunk_id": "aha_acc_cholesterol_2018_p11_c36", "source": "AHA/ACC 2018", "section": "4.4.4.2. Children and Adolescents > Synopsis (section 11)", "excerpt": "4.4.4.2. Children and Adolescents Synopsis Selective screening for lipid disorders on the basis of family history (Recommendation 1) or lifestyle-related factors (Recommendation 2) identifies only a portion of childhood lipid abnormalitiesS4.4.4.2-19,S4.4.4.2-21,S4.4.4.2-26 (Table 9). Values given are in mg/dL. To convert to SI units, divide the…", "guideline_id": "AHA_ACC_CHOLESTEROL_2018", "title": "2018 AHA/ACC/AACVPR/AAPA/ABC/ACPM/ADA/AGS/APhA/ASPC/NLA/PCNA Guideline on the Management of Blood Cholesterol: Executive Summary", "org": "AHA/ACC", "year": 2018, "page": null, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7403606/"}, {"marker": "[3]", "chunk_id": "aha_acc_cholesterol_2018_p11_c28", "source": "AHA/ACC 2018", "section": "4.4.1.1. Risk-Enhancing Factors (section 11)", "excerpt": "4.4.1.1. Risk-Enhancing Factors If measured: 1. Elevated high-sensitivity C-reactive protein (≥2.0 mg/L) 2. Elevated Lp(a): A relative indication for its measurement is family history of premature ASCVD. An Lp(a) ≥50 mg/dL or ≥125 nmol/L constitutes a risk-enhancing factor especially at higher levels of Lp(a). 3. Elevated apoB ≥130 mg/dL: A…", "guideline_id": "AHA_ACC_CHOLESTEROL_2018", "title": "2018 AHA/ACC/AACVPR/AAPA/ABC/ACPM/ADA/AGS/APhA/ASPC/NLA/PCNA Guideline on the Management of Blood Cholesterol: Executive Summary", "org": "AHA/ACC", "year": 2018, "page": null, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7403606/"}], "explanation": {"gate1": {"action": "PROCEED", "rationale": "The patient is asking for general information about cholesterol types.", "missing_fields": [], "source": "llm", "votes": [], "features": {}}, "gate2": {"action": "ANSWER", "rationale": "Chunk 1 directly explains the differences between HDL and LDL cholesterol.", "missing_fields": [], "source": "llm", "votes": [], "features": {"max_rerank": 5.478972434997559, "mean_top3_rerank": 2.9145658214886985, "entity_coverage": 0.3333333333333333, "n_chunks": 5.0}}, "gate3": null, "emergency": {"action": null, "matched_rules": [], "negated": [], "suppressed": [], "bp": null}, "retrieval": [{"rank": 1, "chunk_id": "medlineplus_cholesterol_p2_c1", "guideline_id": "MEDLINEPLUS_CHOLESTEROL", "title": "Cholesterol (MedlinePlus health topic summary)", "org": "NLM MedlinePlus", "year": 2025, "section": "What are HDL, LDL, and VLDL? (section 2)", "page": null, "score_rrf": 0.033333335, "score_rerank": 5.478972434997559, "excerpt": "What are HDL, LDL, and VLDL? HDL, LDL, and VLDL are lipoproteins. They are a combination of fat (lipid) and protein. The lipids need to be attached to the proteins so they can move through the blood. Different types of lipoproteins have different purposes: HDL stands for high-density lipoprotein.…", "cited": true}, {"rank": 2, "chunk_id": "aha_acc_cholesterol_2018_p16_c61", "guideline_id": "AHA_ACC_CHOLESTEROL_2018", "title": "2018 AHA/ACC/AACVPR/AAPA/ABC/ACPM/ADA/AGS/APhA/ASPC/NLA/PCNA Guideline on the Management of Blood Cholesterol: Executive Summary", "org": "AHA/ACC", "year": 2018, "section": "8.2.5. Prognostic Significance of CAC (section 16)", "page": null, "score_rrf": 0.016129032, "score_rerank": 2.2945919036865234, "excerpt": "8.2.5. Prognostic Significance of CAC LDL-C, low-density lipoprotein cholesterol; HDL-C, high-density lipoprotein cholesterol; MI, myocardial infarction; and PCSK9i, PCSK9 inhibitor. Major ASCVD Events Recent ACS (within the past 12 mo) History of MI (other than recent ACS event listed above)…", "cited": false}, {"rank": 3, "chunk_id": "aha_acc_cholesterol_2018_p11_c36", "guideline_id": "AHA_ACC_CHOLESTEROL_2018", "title": "2018 AHA/ACC/