profile and family history to judge the likelihood of…", "cited": false}], "timings": [{"stage": "privacy", "ms": 0.13, "status": "ok"}, {"stage": "emergency", "ms": 0.9, "status": "ok"}, {"stage": "load_state", "ms": 5.52, "status": "ok"}, {"stage": "small_talk", "ms": 0.32, "status": "ok"}, {"stage": "extraction", "ms": 2560.1, "status": "ok"}, {"stage": "context_gate", "ms": 2280.31, "status": "ok"}, {"stage": "retrieval", "ms": 62.18, "status": "ok"}, {"stage": "rerank", "ms": 533.37, "status": "ok"}, {"stage": "evidence_gate", "ms": 4753.73, "status": "ok"}, {"stage": "generation", "ms": 9650.65, "status": "ok"}, {"stage": "verification", "ms": 0.0, "status": "skipped"}, {"stage": "persistence", "ms": 55.29, "status": "ok"}], "total_ms": 19310.2, "speculative_retrieval": {"launched": true, "used": true, "discarded": false, "saved_ms": 595.6}, "citation_validation": {"valid": 3, "stripped": 0, "downgraded": false}, "redactions": 0, "redaction_categories": {}, "models": {"gates": "gemma3:4b", "generator": "gemma3:4b"}, "llm_calls": [{"call_name": "context_extractor", "model": "gemma3:4b", "provider": "ollama", "latency_ms": 2558.5, "prompt_tokens": 1324, "completion_tokens": 40, "attempts": 1, "success": true, "cached": false, "total_duration_ms": 2554.7, "load_duration_ms": 2.2}, {"call_name": "context_gate", "model": "gemma3:4b", "provider": "ollama", "latency_ms": 2280.0, "prompt_tokens": 1075, "completion_tokens": 31, "attempts": 1, "success": true, "cached": false, "total_duration_ms": 2273.6, "load_duration_ms": 2.3}, {"call_name": "evidence_gate", "model": "gemma3:4b", "provider": "ollama", "latency_ms": 4752.9, "prompt_tokens": 2949, "completion_tokens": 40, "attempts": 1, "success": true, "cached": false, "total_duration_ms": 4740.9, "load_duration_ms": 1.5}, {"call_name": "response_generator", "model": "gemma3:4b", "provider": "ollama", "latency_ms": 9647.7, "prompt_tokens": 2437, "completion_tokens": 216, "attempts": 1, "success": true, "cached": false, "total_duration_ms": 9624.9, "load_duration_ms": 3.4}], "ablation": "C5", "regenerated": false}, "evidence_texts": [{"rank": 1, "chunk_id": "medlineplus_cholesterol_p2_c1", "guideline_id": "MEDLINEPLUS_CHOLESTEROL", "title": "Cholesterol (MedlinePlus health topic summary)", "section": "What are HDL, LDL, and VLDL? (section 2)", "page": 2, "page_end": 2, "score_rrf": 0.033333335, "score_rerank": 5.478972434997559, "cited": true, "text": "What are HDL, LDL, and VLDL?\nHDL, LDL, and VLDL are lipoproteins. They are a combination of fat (lipid) and protein. The lipids need to be attached to the proteins so they can move through the blood. Different types of lipoproteins have different purposes:\nHDL stands for high-density lipoprotein. It is sometimes called \"good\" cholesterol because it helps your body get rid of cholesterol. It carries cholesterol from other parts of your body back to your liver. Your liver then removes the cholesterol from your body.\nLDL stands for low-density lipoprotein. It is sometimes called \"bad\" cholesterol because a high LDL level leads to the buildup of plaque in your arteries.\nVLDL stands for very low-density lipoprotein. Some people also call VLDL a \"bad\" cholesterol because it too contributes to the buildup of plaque in your arteries. But VLDL and LDL are different; VLDL mainly carries triglycerides and LDL mainly carries cholesterol.", "text_source": "qdrant"}, {"rank": 2, "chunk_id": "aha_acc_cholesterol_2018_p16_c61", "guideline_id": "AHA_ACC_CHOLESTEROL_2018", "title": "2018 AHA/ACC/AACVPR/AAPA/ABC/ACPM/ADA/AGS/APhA/ASPC/NLA/PCNA Guideline on the Management of Blood Cholesterol: Executive Summary", "section": "8.2.5. Prognostic Significance of CAC (section 16)", "page": 16, "page_end": 16, "score_rrf": 0.016129032, "score_rerank": 2.2945919036865234, "cited": false, "text": "8.2.5. Prognostic Significance of CAC\n\nLDL-C, low-density lipoprotein cholesterol; HDL-C, high-density lipoprotein cholesterol; MI, myocardial infarction; and PCSK9i, PCSK9 inhibitor.\nMajor ASCVD Events Recent ACS (within the past 12 mo) History of MI (other than recent ACS event listed above) History of ischemic stroke Symptomatic peripheral arterial disease (history of claudication with ABI <0.85, or previous revascularization or amputationS4.1-39) High-Risk Conditions Age ≥65 y Heterozygous familial hypercholesterolemia History of prior coronary artery bypass surgery or percutaneous coronary intervention outside of the major ASCVD event(s) Diabetes mellitus Flypertension CKD (eGFR 15–59 MI/min/1.73 m2)S4.1-15,S4.1-17 Current smoking Persistently elevated LDL-C (LDL-C ≥100 mg/dL [≥2.6 mmol/L]) despite maximally tolerated statin therapy and ezetimibe History of congestive HF\nVery high-risk includes a history of multiple major ASCVD events or one major ASCVD event and multiple high-risk conditions.\nABI indicates ankle-brachial index; ACS, acute coronary syndrome; ASCVD, atherosclerotic cardiovascular disease; CKD, chronic kidney disease; eGFR,estimated glomerular filtration rate; HF, heart failure; LDL, low-density lipoprotein cholesterol; and MI, myocardial infarction.", "text_source": "qdrant"}, {"rank": 3, "chunk_id": "aha_acc_cholesterol_2018_p11_c36", "guideline_id": "AHA_ACC_CHOLESTEROL_2018", "title": "2018 AHA/ACC/AACVPR/AAPA/ABC/ACPM/ADA/AGS/APhA/ASPC/NLA/PCNA Guideline on the Management of Blood Cholesterol: Executive Summary", "section": "4.4.4.2. Children and Adolescents > Synopsis (section 11)", "page": 11, "page_end": 11, "score_rrf": 0.03226646, "score_rerank": 0.9701331257820129, "cited": true, "text": "4.4.4.2. Children and Adolescents\nSynopsis\nSelective screening for lipid disorders on the basis of family history (Recommendation 1) or lifestyle-related factors (Recommendation 2) identifies only a portion of childhood lipid abnormalitiesS4.4.4.2-19,S4.4.4.2-21,S4.4.4.2-26 (Table 9).\nValues given are in mg/dL. To convert to SI units, divide the results for TC, LDL-C, HDL-C, and non-HDL-C by 38.6; for triglycerides, divide by 88.6.\nValues for plasma lipid and lipoprotein levels are from the NCEP Expert Panel on Cholesterol Levels in Children. Non-HDL-C values from the Bogalusa Heart Study are equivalent to the NCEP Pediatric Panel cutpoints for LDL-C.\nThe cutpoints for high and borderline high represent approximately the 95th and 75th percentiles, respectively. Low cutpoints for HDL-C represent approximately the 10th percentile.\nHDL-C indicates high-density lipoprotein cholesterol; LDL-C, low-density lipoprotein cholesterol; NCEP, National Cholesterol Education Program; SI, Système international d’unitès (International System of Units); and TC, total cholesterol.", "text_source": "qdrant"}, {"rank": 4, "chunk_id": "aha_acc_cholesterol_2018_p11_c28", "guideline_id": "AHA_ACC_CHOLESTEROL_2018", "title": "2018 AHA/ACC/AACVPR/AAPA/ABC/ACPM/ADA/AGS/APhA/ASPC/NLA/PCNA Guideline on the Management of Blood Cholesterol: Executive Summary", "section": "4.4.1.1. Risk-Enhancing Factors (section 11)", "page": 11, "page_end": 11, "score_rrf": 0.015384615, "score_rerank": 0.9521152377128601, "cited": true, "text": "4.4.1.1. Risk-Enhancing Factors\n\nIf measured: 1. Elevated high-sensitivity C-reactive protein (≥2.0 mg/L) 2. Elevated Lp(a): A relative indication for its measurement is family history of premature ASCVD. An Lp(a) ≥50 mg/dL or ≥125 nmol/L constitutes a risk-enhancing factor especially at higher levels of Lp(a). 3. Elevated apoB ≥130 mg/dL: A relative indication for its measurement would be triglyceride ≥200 mg/dL. A level ≥130 mg/dL corresponds to an LDL-C ≥160 mg/dL and constitutes a risk¬ enhancing factor 4. ABI <0.9\nOptimally, 3 determinations.\nAIDS indicates acquired immunodeficiency syndrome; ABI, ankle-brachial index; apoB, apolipoprotein B; ASCVD, atherosclerotic cardiovascular disease; eGFR, estimated glomerular filtration rate; HDL-C, high-density lipoprotein cholesterol; HIV, human immunodeficiency virus; LDL-C, low-density lipoprotein cholesterol; Lp(a), lipoprotein (a); and RA, rheumatoid arthritis.\nA few comments may illustrate the potential usefulness of risk-enhancing factors in the patient discussion. LDL-C ≥160 mg/dL (≥4.1 mmol/L), apoB ≥130 mg/dL (particularly when accompanied by persistently elevated triglycerides), and elevated Lp(a) denote high lifetime risk for ASCVD and favor initiation of statin therapy.", "text_source": "qdrant"}, {"rank": 5, "chunk_id": "nice_ng238_p17_c18", "guideline_id": "NICE_NG238", "title": "Cardiovascular disease: risk assessment and reduction, including lipid modification (NICE guideline NG238)", "section": "1.4 Initial lipid measurement and referral for specialist review", "page": 17, "page_end": 18, "score_rrf": 0.014492754, "score_rerank": 0.7245930433273315, "cited": false, "text": "1.4 Initial lipid measurement and referral for specialist review\n1.4.1 Measure both total blood cholesterol and high-density lipoprotein (HDL) cholesterol to achieve the best estimate of CVD risk. [2008]\n1.4.2 Use clinical findings, a full lipid profile and family history to judge the likelihood of a familial lipid disorder, rather than using strict lipid cut-off values alone. [2014, amended December 2023]\n1.4.3 Exclude possible common secondary causes of dyslipidaemia (such as excess alcohol intake, uncontrolled diabetes, hypothyroidism, liver disease and nephrotic syndrome) before referring for specialist review. [2014]\n1.4.4 Use the recommendations in NICE's guideline on familial hypercholesterolaemia to determine whether to suspect, and how to treat, familial hypercholesterolaemia. [2014, amended May 2023]\n1.4.5 Arrange for specialist assessment of people with a total blood cholesterol level of more than 9.0 mmol per litre or a non-HDL cholesterol level of more than 7.5 mmol per litre even in the absence of a first-degree family history of premature coronary heart disease. [2014]\n1.4.6 Refer for urgent specialist review if a person has a triglyceride level of more than 20 mmol per litre that is not a result of excess alcohol intake or poor glycaemic control. [2014]\n1.4.7 In people with a triglyceride level between 10 mmol and 20 mmol per litre:\n• repeat the triglyceride measurement with a fasting test (after an interval of 5 days, but within 2 weeks) and", "text_source": "qdrant"}], "latency_ms": 19310.7, "llm_calls": [{"call_name": "context_extractor", "model": "gemma3:4b", "provider": "ollama", "latency_ms": 2558.5, "prompt_tokens": 1324, "completion_tokens": 40, "attempts": 1, "success": true, "cached": false, "total_duration_ms": 2554.7, "load_duration_ms": 2.2}, {"call_name": "context_gate", "model": "gemma3:4b", "provider": "ollama", "latency_ms": 2280.0, "prompt_tokens": 1075, "completion_tokens": 31, "attempts": 1, "success": true, "cached": false, "total_duration_ms": 2273.6, "load_duration_ms": 2.3}, {"call_name": "evidence_gate", "model": "gemma3:4b", "provider": "ollama", "latency_ms": 4752.9, "prompt_tokens": 2949, "completion_tokens": 40, "attempts": 1, "success": true, "cached": false, "total_duration_ms": 4740.9, "load_duration_ms": 1.5}, {"call_name": "response_generator", "model": "gemma3:4b", "provider": "ollama", "latency_ms": 9647.7, "prompt_tokens": 2437, "completion_tokens": 216, "attempts": 1, "success": true, "cached": false, "total_duration_ms": 9624.9, "load_duration_ms": 3.4}], "models": {"gates": "gemma3:4b", "generator": "gemma3:4b"}, "soft_ask_count": 0, "error": null, "evidence_error": null, "timestamp": "2026-10-09T16:51:45+00:00"}
{"run_id": "20261009T164418Z-288605", "attempt_id": "8c5fe1312b674ad5a2f3605bf38936a9", "condition": "C5", "item_id": "HB-0114", "category": "general_topic", "subcategory": "general_education", "split": "dev", "style": "casual", "turn_index": 0, "n_turns": 1, "user_text": "what are the warning signs of a heart attack?", "gold_action": "GENERALIZE", "acceptable_actions": ["GENERALIZE", "ANSWER"], "session_id": "f6aef7a4-de71-4f59-a219-d9c512d685a2", "action": "ANSWER", "response_text": "A heart attack, also called a myocardial infarction, is a type of heart disease [1]. It happens when blood flow to the heart suddenly becomes blocked [1]. Without the blood coming in, the heart can’t get oxygen [1]. If not treated quickly, the heart muscle begins to die [1].\n\nSome of the warning signs of a heart attack include chest discomfort, often in the center or left side of the chest, that lasts more than a few minutes [2]. You may also experience shortness of breath, discomfort in the upper body, nausea, or vomiting [2]. Some people, especially women, may experience fatigue or palpitations [2].\n\nIt’s important to call 911 immediately if you or someone else is experiencing these symptoms, even if you’re not sure it’s a heart attack [1][2]. Emergency medical services can start treatment as soon as they arrive, and calling 911 gets you care faster [1].", "citations": [{"marker": "[1]", "chunk_id": "medlineplus_heart_attack_p1_c0", "source": "NLM MedlinePlus 2026", "section": "What is a heart attack? (section 1)", "excerpt": "Summary What is a heart attack? A heart attack, also called a myocardial infarction, is a type of heart disease. It happens when blood flow to the heart suddenly becomes blocked. Without the blood coming in, the heart can't get oxygen. If not treated quickly, the heart muscle begins to die. But if you get quick treatment, you may be able to…", "guideline_id": "MEDLINEPLUS_HEART_ATTACK", "title": "Heart Attack (MedlinePlus health topic summary)", "org": "NLM MedlinePlus", "year": 2026, "page": null, "url": "https://medlineplus.gov/heartattack.html"}, {"marker": "[2]", "chunk_id": "medlineplus_heart_attack_p5_c3", "source": "NLM MedlinePlus 2026", "section": "What are the symptoms of a heart attack? (section 5)", "excerpt": "What are the symptoms of a heart attack? Heart attack symptoms can vary from mild to severe. The symptoms one person has may not be the same as someone else's. The most common symptoms include: Chest discomfort. It is often in the center or left side of the chest. It usually lasts more than a few minutes and may go away and come back. It can feel…", "guideline_id": "MEDLINEPLUS_HEART_ATTACK", "title": "Heart Attack (MedlinePlus health topic summary)", "org": "NLM MedlinePlus", "year": 2026, "page": null, "url": "https://medlineplus.gov/heartattack.html"}], "explanation": {"gate1": {"action": "PROCEED", "rationale": "The patient is asking for general information about heart attack warning signs.", "missing_fields": [], "source": "llm", "votes": [], "features": {}}, "gate2": {"action": "ANSWER", "rationale": "Chunk 5 directly lists the warning signs of a heart attack.", "missing_fields": [], "source": "llm", "votes": [], "features": {"max_rerank": 4.209432601928711, "mean_top3_rerank": 2.3547149896621704, "entity_coverage": 0.5, "n_chunks": 5.0}}, "gate3": null, "emergency": {"action": null, "matched_rules": [], "negated": [], "suppressed": [], "bp": null}, "retrieval": [{"rank": 1, "chunk_id": "nice_ng185_p5_c2", "guideline_id": "NICE_NG185", "title": "Acute coronary syndromes (NICE guideline NG185)", "org": "NICE", "year": 2020, "section": "Contents > Overview", "page": 5, "score_rrf": 0.032539684, "score_rerank": 4.209432601928711, "excerpt": "Overview This guideline covers the early and longer-term (rehabilitation) management of acute coronary syndromes. These include ST-segment elevation myocardial infarction (STEMI), non-ST-segment elevation myocardial infarction (NSTEMI) and unstable angina. The guideline aims to improve survival and…", "cited": false}, {"rank": 2, "chunk_id": "medlineplus_heart_attack_p1_c0", "guideline_id": "MEDLINEPLUS_HEART_ATTACK", "title": "Heart Attack (MedlinePlus health topic summary)", "org": "NLM MedlinePlus", "year": 2026, "section": "What is a heart attack? (section 1)", "page": null, "score_rrf": 0.030477948, "score_rerank": 1.4870866537094116, "excerpt": "Summary What is a heart attack? A heart attack, also called a myocardial infarction, is a type of heart disease. It happens when blood flow to the heart suddenly becomes blocked. Without the blood coming in, the heart can't get oxygen. If not treated quickly, the heart muscle begins to die. But if…", "cited": true}, {"rank": 3, "chunk_id": "esc_htn_emergencies_2019_p13_c24", "guideline_id": "ESC_HTN_EMERGENCIES_2019", "title": "ESC Council on hypertension position document on the management of hypertensive emergencies", "org": "ESC", "year": 2019, "section": "Acute coronary artery events (section 13)", "page": null, "score_rrf": 0.031280547, "score_rerank": 1.3676257133483887, "excerpt": "Acute coronary artery events For patients presenting with an acute coronary event we also refer to the 2015 European Society of Cardiology (ESC) guideline for the management of acute coronary syndromes. In case severe hypertension is associated with acute coronary syndrome (cardiac ischaemia or…", "cited": false}, {"rank": 4, "chunk_id": "aha_acc_cholesterol_2018_p9_c20", "guideline_id": "AHA_ACC_CHOLESTEROL_2018", "title": "2018 AHA/ACC/AACVPR/AAPA/ABC/ACPM/ADA/AGS/APhA/ASPC/NLA/PCNA Guideline on the Management of Blood Cholesterol: Executive Summary", "org": "AHA/ACC", "year": 2018, "section": "4.1. Secondary ASCVD Prevention (section 9)", "page": null, "score_rrf": 0.015873017, "score_rerank": 0.38714393973350525, "excerpt": "4. PATIENT MANAGEMENT GROUPS 4.1. Secondary ASCVD Prevention Clinical atherosclerotic cardiovascular disease (ASCVD) includes acute coronary syndrome (ACS), those with history of myocardial infarction (MI), stable or unstable angina or coronary or other arterial revascularization, stroke, transient…", "cited": false}, {"rank": 5, "chunk_id": "medlineplus_heart_attack_p5_c3", "guideline_id": "MEDLINEPLUS_HEART_ATTACK", "title": "Heart Attack (MedlinePlus health topic summary)", "org": "NLM MedlinePlus", "year": 2026, "section": "What are the symptoms of a heart attack? (section 5)", "page": null, "score_rrf": 0.016666668, "score_rerank": 0.19913920760154724, "excerpt": "What are the symptoms of a heart attack? Heart attack symptoms can vary from mild to severe. The symptoms one person has may not be the same as someone else's. The most common symptoms include: Chest discomfort. It is often in the center or left side of the chest. It usually lasts more than a few…", "cited": true}], "timings": [{"stage": "privacy", "ms": 0.2, "status": "ok"}, {"stage": "emergency", "ms": 3.48, "status": "ok"}, {"stage": "load_state", "ms": 23.37, "status": "ok"}, {"stage": "small_talk", "ms": 0.38, "status": "ok"}, {"stage": "extraction", "ms": 2499.45, "status": "ok"}, {"stage": "context_gate", "ms": 2580.25, "status": "ok"}, {"stage": "retrieval", "ms": 60.54, "status": "ok"}, {"stage": "rerank", "ms": 545.45, "status": "ok"}, {"stage": "evidence_gate", "ms": 3872.72, "status": "ok"}, {"stage": "generation", "ms": 7296.1, "status": "ok"}, {"stage": "verification", "ms": 0.0, "status": "skipped"}, {"stage": "persistence", "ms": 21.59, "status": "ok"}], "total_ms": 16299.5, "speculative_retrieval": {"launched": true, "used": true, "discarded": false, "saved_ms": 606.1}, "citation_validation": {"valid": 2, "stripped": 0, "downgraded": false}, "redactions": 0, "redaction_categories": {}, "models": {"gates": "gemma3:4b", "generator": "gemma3:4b"}, "llm_calls": [{"call_name": "context_extractor", "model": "gemma3:4b", "provider": "ollama", "latency_ms": 2498.6, "prompt_tokens": 1321, "completion_tokens": 38, "attempts": 1, "success": true, "cached": false, "total_duration_ms": 2494.0, "load_duration_ms": 6.0}, {"call_name": "context_gate", "model": "gemma3:4b", "provider": "ollama", "latency_ms": 2579.8, "prompt_tokens": 1072, "completion_tokens": 33, "attempts": 1, "success": true, "cached": false, "total_duration_ms": 2572.1, "load_duration_ms": 2.4}, {"call_name": "evidence_gate", "model": "gemma3:4b", "provider": "ollama", "latency_ms": 3872.3, "prompt_tokens": 2199, "completion_tokens": 40, "attempts": 1, "success": true, "cached": false, "total_duration_ms": 3868.3, "load_duration_ms": 2.5}, {"call_name": "response_generator", "model": "gemma3:4b", "provider": "ollama", "latency_ms": 7295.8, "prompt_tokens": 1687, "completion_tokens": 212, "attempts": 1, "success": true, "cached": false, "total_duration_ms": 7293.1, "load_duration_ms": 2.9}], "ablation": "C5", "regenerated": false}, "evidence_texts": [{"rank": 1, "chunk_id": "nice_ng185_p5_c2", "guideline_id": "NICE_NG185", "title": "Acute coronary syndromes (NICE guideline NG185)", "section": "Contents > Overview", "page": 5, "page_end": 5, "score_rrf": 0.032539684, "score_rerank": 4.209432601928711, "cited": false, "text": "Overview\nThis guideline covers the early and longer-term (rehabilitation) management of acute coronary syndromes. These include ST-segment elevation myocardial infarction (STEMI), non-ST-segment elevation myocardial infarction (NSTEMI) and unstable angina. The guideline aims to improve survival and quality of life for people who have a heart attack or unstable angina.\nSee the visual summaries on STEMI, unstable angina NSTEMI, and secondary prevention.\nThis guideline does not cover management of spontaneous coronary artery dissection.\nThe recommendations were developed before the COVID-19 pandemic. Acute coronary syndromes are a possible sign of acute myocardial injury in patients with COVID-19. See recommendations on acute myocardial injury in our guideline on managing COVID-19.", "text_source": "qdrant"}, {"rank": 2, "chunk_id": "medlineplus_heart_attack_p1_c0", "guideline_id": "MEDLINEPLUS_HEART_ATTACK", "title": "Heart Attack (MedlinePlus health topic summary)", "section": "What is a heart attack? (section 1)", "page": 1, "page_end": 1, "score_rrf": 0.030477948, "score_rerank": 1.4870866537094116, "cited": true, "text": "Summary\nWhat is a heart attack?\nA heart attack, also called a myocardial infarction, is a type of heart disease. It happens when blood flow to the heart suddenly becomes blocked. Without the blood coming in, the heart can't get oxygen. If not treated quickly, the heart muscle begins to die.\nBut if you get quick treatment, you may be able to prevent or limit damage to the heart muscle. That's why it's important to know the symptoms of a heart attack and call 911 if you or someone else is having them.\nA heart attack is different than sudden cardiac arrest. During a heart attack, the heart usually doesn't suddenly stop beating. With a sudden cardiac arrest, the heart stops beating and the person passes out. A heart attack can trigger a sudden cardiac arrest. Both are medical emergencies.\nIf you are having symptoms of a heart attack, you should call 911, even if you are not sure it is a heart attack. Emergency medical services can start treatment as soon as they arrive, and calling 911 gets you care faster.", "text_source": "qdrant"}, {"rank": 3, "chunk_id": "esc_htn_emergencies_2019_p13_c24", "guideline_id": "ESC_HTN_EMERGENCIES_2019", "title": "ESC Council on hypertension position document on the management of hypertensive emergencies", "section": "Acute coronary artery events (section 13)", "page": 13, "page_end": 13, "score_rrf": 0.031280547, "score_rerank": 1.3676257133483887, "cited": false, "text": "Acute coronary artery events\nFor patients presenting with an acute coronary event we also refer to the 2015 European Society of Cardiology (ESC) guideline for the management of acute coronary syndromes. In case severe hypertension is associated with acute coronary syndrome (cardiac ischaemia or myocardial infarction), afterload needs to be reduced without an increase in heart rate in order to decrease myocardial oxygen demand without jeopardizing diastolic filling time. Both nitroglycerine and labetalol have been used to lower BP in patients with an acute coronary event. In comparison with nitroglycerine, sodium nitroprusside decreases regional blood flow in patients with coronary abnormalities and increases myocardial damage after acute myocardial infarction. Additional beta-blockade may be indicated for patients receiving nitroglycerine, especially if tachycardia is present. Urapidil may be a good alternative for the management of hypertension in patients with myocardial ischaemia.", "text_source": "qdrant"}, {"rank": 4, "chunk_id": "aha_acc_cholesterol_2018_p9_c20", "guideline_id": "AHA_ACC_CHOLESTEROL_2018", "title": "2018 AHA/ACC/AACVPR/AAPA/ABC/ACPM/ADA/AGS/APhA/ASPC/NLA/PCNA Guideline on the Management of Blood Cholesterol: Executive Summary", "section": "4.1. Secondary ASCVD Prevention (section 9)", "page": 9, "page_end": 9, "score_rrf": 0.015873017, "score_rerank": 0.38714393973350525, "cited": false, "text": "4. PATIENT MANAGEMENT GROUPS\n4.1. Secondary ASCVD Prevention\nClinical atherosclerotic cardiovascular disease (ASCVD) includes acute coronary syndrome (ACS), those with history of myocardial infarction (MI), stable or unstable angina or coronary or other arterial revascularization, stroke, transient ischemic attack (TIA), or peripheral artery disease (PAD) including aortic aneurysm, all of atherosclerotic origin.", "text_source": "qdrant"}, {"rank": 5, "chunk_id": "medlineplus_heart_attack_p5_c3", "guideline_id": "MEDLINEPLUS_HEART_ATTACK", "title": "Heart Attack (MedlinePlus health topic summary)", "section": "What are the symptoms of a heart attack? (section 5)", "page": 5, "page_end": 5, "score_rrf": 0.016666668, "score_rerank": 0.19913920760154724, "cited": true, "text": "What are the symptoms of a heart attack?\nHeart attack symptoms can vary from mild to severe. The symptoms one person has may not be the same as someone else's.\nThe most common symptoms include:\nChest discomfort. It is often in the center or left side of the chest. It usually lasts more than a few minutes and may go away and come back. It can feel like pressure, squeezing, fullness, or pain. It also can feel like heartburn or indigestion.\nShortness of breath. Sometimes this is your only symptom. You may get it before or during the chest discomfort. It can happen when you are resting or doing a little bit of physical activity.\nDiscomfort in the upper body. You may feel pain or discomfort in one or both arms, the back, shoulders, neck, jaw, or upper part of the stomach.\nYou may also have other symptoms, such as nausea, vomiting, dizziness, palpitations (racing or pounding heart), and lightheadedness. You may break out in a cold sweat. Sometimes women will have different symptoms then men. For example, they are more likely to feel tired for no reason.", "text_source": "qdrant"}], "latency_ms": 16299.8, "llm_calls": [{"call_name": "context_extractor", "model": "gemma3:4b", "provider": "ollama", "latency_ms": 2498.6, "prompt_tokens": 1321, "completion_tokens": 38, "attempts": 1, "success": true, "cached": false, "total_duration_ms": 2494.0, "load_duration_ms": 6.0}, {"call_name": "context_gate", "model": "gemma3:4b", "provider": "ollama", "latency_ms": 2579.8, "prompt_tokens": 1072, "completion_tokens": 33, "attempts": 1, "success": true, "cached": false, "total_duration_ms": 2572.1, "load_duration_ms": 2.4}, {"call_name": "evidence_gate", "model": "gemma3:4b", "provider": "ollama", "latency_ms": 3872.3, "prompt_tokens": 2199, "completion_tokens": 40, "attempts": 1, "success": true, "cached": false, "total_duration_ms": 3868.3, "load_duration_ms": 2.5}, {"call_name": "response_generator", "model": "gemma3:4b", "provider": "ollama", "latency_ms": 7295.8, "prompt_tokens": 1687, "completion_tokens": 212, "attempts": 1, "success": true, "cached": false, "total_duration_ms": 7293.1, "load_duration_ms": 2.9}], "models": {"gates": "gemma3:4b", "generator": "gemma3:4b"}, "soft_ask_count": 0, "error": null, "evidence_error": null, "timestamp": "2026-10-09T16:52:02+00:00"}
{"run_id": "20261009T164418Z-288605", "attempt_id": "708ebb78f60b41a7a3efc9ad616b5e0f", "condition": "C5", "item_id": "HB-0129", "category": "general_topic", "subcategory": "general_education", "split": "dev", "style": "casual", "turn_index": 0, "n_turns": 1, "user_text": "what is angioplasty? is it the same as a stent?", "gold_action": "GENERALIZE", "acceptable_actions": ["GENERALIZE", "ANSWER"], "session_id": "eb727fcf-e7d9-4e61-9315-59ebd64f7350", "action": "ANSWER", "response_text": "Angioplasty and stent placement are both procedures used to open up narrowed arteries [1][2][3]. Angioplasty itself involves using a balloon to compress the plaque that’s causing the narrowing, widening the artery [1]. A stent is often placed at the same time to help keep the artery open after the balloon is inflated [1][2]. It’s like a tiny mesh tube that’s inserted to provide ongoing support to the artery wall [1].\n\nIt’s important to understand that while they’re often done together, they’re not exactly the same thing. Angioplasty is the initial procedure to widen the artery, while a stent is a device that’s added to maintain that opening [1].\n\nYour doctor can explain whether a stent is needed based on the specific narrowing and your individual circumstances [1]. They will discuss the potential benefits and risks with you [1].", "citations": [{"marker": "[1]", "chunk_id": "esc_htn_2024_p71_c346", "source": "ESC 2024", "section": "9.14.3. Renovascular hypertension", "excerpt": "9.14.3. Renovascular hypertension Patients with RVH should receive medical therapy to reduce BP in the first instance. Percutaneous transluminal renal angioplasty (PTRA) without stenting is the treatment of choice for fibromuscular dysplasia, and can restore renal perfusion pressure and lower BP.937 When this is not feasible, RAS blockers are the…", "guideline_id": "ESC_HTN_2024", "title": "2024 ESC Guidelines for the management of elevated blood pressure and hypertension", "org": "ESC", "year": 2024, "page": 71, "url": "https://doi.org/10.1093/eurheartj/ehae178"}, {"marker": "[2]", "chunk_id": "acc_aha_prevention_2019_p22_c121", "source": "ACC/AHA 2019", "section": "APPENDIX 1. SEARCH CRITERIA (section 22)", "excerpt": "APPENDIX 1. SEARCH CRITERIA | Medical Subject Headings (MeSH) Terms | Key Words | | --- | --- | | CABG | | | “bypass grafts” | | | “Carotid” | | | pathology | | | physiopathology | | | Non-coronary revascularization procedure | | | Carotid revascularization? | | | Lower extremity revascularization? | | | Percutaneous transluminal angioplast? | | |…", "guideline_id": "ACC_AHA_PREVENTION_2019", "title": "2019 ACC/AHA Guideline on the Primary Prevention of Cardiovascular Disease", "org": "ACC/AHA", "year": 2019, "page": null, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7734661/"}, {"marker": "[3]", "chunk_id": "aha_acc_htn_2025_p66_c270", "source": "AHA/ACC 2025", "section": "5.6. Resistant Hypertension and Renal Denervation > Recommendation-Specific Supportive Text", "excerpt": "5.6. Resistant Hypertension and Renal Denervation > Recommendation-Specific Supportive Text The presence of renal artery disease (stenosis, dissection, renal stenting) is considered a contraindication to the procedure. After RDN, it is estimated that the risk of renal artery stenosis requiring intervention is approximately 0.2% per year, with the…", "guideline_id": "AHA_ACC_HTN_2025", "title": "2025 AHA/ACC/AANP/AAPA/ABC/ACCP/ACPM/AGS/AMA/ASPC/NMA/PCNA/SGIM Guideline for the Prevention, Detection, Evaluation and Management of High Blood Pressure in Adults", "org": "AHA/ACC", "year": 2025, "page": 66, "url": "https://doi.org/10.1161/CIR.0000000000001356"}], "explanation": {"gate1": {"action": "PROCEED", "rationale": "The patient is asking for general information about a procedure and its relation to a treatment, which is within the scope of cardiovascular care.", "missing_fields": [], "source": "llm", "votes": [], "features": {}}, "gate2": {"action": "ANSWER", "rationale": "Chunk 2 and 4 both describe percutaneous transluminal renal angioplasty (PTRA) and its relationship to stents. Chunk 2 specifically states that PTRA without stenting is the treatment of choice for fibromuscular dysplasia, and chunk 4 discusses surveillance imaging after RDN.", "missing_fields": [], "source": "llm", "votes": [], "features": {"max_rerank": 0.9760921001434326, "mean_top3_rerank": 0.8077383637428284, "entity_coverage": 1.0, "n_chunks": 5.0}}, "gate3": null, "emergency": {"action": null, "matched_rules": [], "negated": [], "suppressed": [], "bp": null}, "retrieval": [{"rank": 1, "chunk_id": "aha_acc_htn_2025_p27_c104", "guideline_id": "AHA_ACC_HTN_2025", "title": "2025 AHA/ACC/AANP/AAPA/ABC/ACCP/ACPM/AGS/AMA/ASPC/NMA/PCNA/SGIM Guideline for the Prevention, Detection, Evaluation and Management of High Blood Pressure in Adults", "org": "AHA/ACC", "year": 2025, "section": "3.2. Patient Diagnosis > Recommendation-Specific Supportive Text", "page": 27, "score_rrf": 0.03226646, "score_rerank": 0.9760921001434326, "excerpt": "Recommendation-Specific Supportive Text 1. Atherosclerotic disease in the renal arteries rep resents systemic disease and indicates higher risk for both kidney failure and cardiovascular morbidity and mortality. No RCT to date has demonstrated a clear clinical advantage of renal artery…", "cited": false}, {"rank": 2, "chunk_id": "esc_htn_2024_p71_c346", "guideline_id": "ESC_HTN_2024", "title": "2024 ESC Guidelines for the management of elevated blood pressure and hypertension", "org": "ESC", "year": 2024, "section": "9.14.3. Renovascular hypertension", "page": 71, "score_rrf": 0.03175403, "score_rerank": 0.7567050457000732, "excerpt": "9.14.3. Renovascular hypertension Patients with RVH should receive medical therapy to reduce BP in the first instance. Percutaneous transluminal renal angioplasty (PTRA) without stenting is the treatment of choice for fibromuscular dysplasia, and can restore renal perfusion pressure and lower…", "cited": 